# What not to upload to Vercel.
#
# `vercel deploy --archive=tgz` packs the working directory, and without this it
# packed all of it: 1.2 GB per deploy against 4.7 MB of actual source. None of
# the difference was ever used — Vercel runs `pnpm install` and builds from
# source on its side, so every byte of `node_modules` and every prebuilt `.next`
# was uploaded, ignored, and thrown away.
#
# Six projects deploy on every green pipeline, so that waste was paid six times
# a merge. It is also why deploys took minutes and why the Hobby plan's daily
# deploy limit hurt as much as it did.
#
# This file is not `.gitignore`. A deploy needs things git does not track (an
# untracked `.env.production` on a machine that has one) and must not need
# things git does track. Keeping them separate means neither surprises the
# other.

# Reinstalled by Vercel from the lockfile. The largest single entry by far.
node_modules

# Build output, rebuilt by Vercel. Uploading it neither speeds the build up nor
# gets used — and a stale local build being present is worse than absent,
# because it looks like it might be what shipped.
.next
.turbo
dist
.vercel/output
storybook-static

# History. Vercel builds a snapshot, not a repository.
.git

# Local noise.
*.log
coverage
test-results
playwright-report
.DS_Store
